# NestJS Project Guidelines

- Use yarn as package manager
- Always follow guidelines in [typescript-guidelines.md](mdc:.cursor/rules/typescript-guidelines.md)
- Refer to [project_structure.md](mdc:.cursor/rules/backend/project_structure.md) to know the current project structure and update the files according to your modifications

## Rule 1: GraphQL Query and Mutation Naming

**Description:** Each GraphQL query or mutation should have an explicit name that clearly describes its purpose, instead of relying on the function name.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
@Query(() => String, { name: 'getHello' })
async hello(): Promise<string> {
  return 'Hello, world!';
}
```

**Bad Example:**
```typescript
@Query(() => String, { name: 'hello' })
async hello(): Promise<string> {
  return 'Hello, world!';
}
```

## Rule 2: DTOs and Input Types Structure

**Description:** Arguments passed through queries or mutations must be defined in a dedicated DTO file within the same-level `dtos` folder. Args should have a data field defined by an input type.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
// hello.dto.ts
@InputType()
export class HelloDto {
  @Field(() => String)
  data: string;
}

@ArgsType()
export class HelloArgs {
  @Field(() => HelloDto)
  @Type(() => HelloDto)
  @ValidateNested()
  data: HelloDto;
}

// hello.resolver.ts
@Query(() => String, { name: 'getHello' })
async hello(@Args() args: HelloArgs): Promise<string> {
  return this.helloService.hello(args.data);
}
```

**Bad Example:**
```typescript
@Query(() => String)
async hello(@Args('message') message: string): Promise<string> {
  return this.helloService.hello(message);
}
```

## Rule 3: Authentication and User Context

**Description:** Utilize the @AuthUser() decorator to access user context instead of requiring user information in arguments.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
@Query(() => String, { name: 'getHello' })
async hello(@AuthUser() user: User): Promise<string> {
  return this.helloService.hello(user.id);
}
```

**Bad Example:**
```typescript
@Query(() => String)
async hello(@Args('userId') userId: string): Promise<string> {
  return this.helloService.hello(userId);
}
```

## Rule 4: Service Layer Pattern

**Description:** Implement business logic in services, keeping resolvers thin and focused on GraphQL operations.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
// hello.service.ts
@Injectable()
export class HelloService {
  async hello(userId: string): Promise<string> {
    // Business logic here
    return `Hello ${userId}`;
  }
}

// hello.resolver.ts
@Resolver()
export class HelloResolver {
  constructor(private readonly helloService: HelloService) {}

  @Query(() => String, { name: 'getHello' })
  async hello(@AuthUser() user: User): Promise<string> {
    return this.helloService.hello(user.id);
  }
}
```

**Bad Example:**
```typescript
@Resolver()
export class HelloResolver {
  @Query(() => String)
  async hello(@AuthUser() user: User): Promise<string> {
    // Business logic in resolver
    const greeting = await someComplexOperation(user.id);
    return greeting;
  }
}
```

## Rule 5: Prisma Repository Pattern

**Description:** Use repository pattern with Prisma to encapsulate database operations and make them reusable.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
@Injectable()
export class UserRepository {
  constructor(private prisma: PrismaService) {}

  async findById(id: string): Promise<User> {
    return this.prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { id } });
  }
}
```

**Bad Example:**
```typescript
@Injectable()
export class UserService {
  constructor(private prisma: PrismaService) {}

  async getUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
    // Direct Prisma calls in service
    return this.prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { id } });
  }
}
```

## Rule 6: Redis Caching Strategy

**Description:** Implement caching with proper TTL and cache invalidation strategies. Cache key should follow the [Rule 11](mdc:#Rule11)

**Good Example:**
```typescript
@Injectable()
export class UserService {
  @CacheKey(CACHE_KEY.USER)
  @CacheTTL(3600)
  async getUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
    return this.userRepository.findById(id);
  }
}
```

**Bad Example:**
```typescript
@Injectable()
export class UserService {
  @Cache()
  async getUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
    return this.userRepository.findById(id);
  }
}
```

## Rule 7: Error Handling

**Description:** Use custom exception filters and proper error types for GraphQL errors.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
@Catch(PrismaError)
export class PrismaExceptionFilter implements GqlExceptionFilter {
  catch(exception: PrismaError, host: ArgumentsHost) {
    const gqlHost = GqlArgumentsHost.create(host);
    return new GraphQLError('Database error', {
      extensions: { code: 'DATABASE_ERROR' },
    });
  }
}
```

**Bad Example:**
```typescript
try {
  await this.prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { id } });
} catch (error) {
  throw new Error('Something went wrong');
}
```

## Rule 8: Validation Pipes

**Description:** Use validation pipes with proper validation decorators in DTOs.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
@InputType()
export class CreateUserDto {
  @Field()
  @IsEmail()
  @IsNotEmpty()
  email: string;

  @Field()
  @MinLength(8)
  @Matches(/^(?=.*[A-Za-z])(?=.*\d)[A-Za-z\d]{8,}$/)
  password: string;
}
```

**Bad Example:**
```typescript
@InputType()
export class CreateUserDto {
  @Field()
  email: string;

  @Field()
  password: string;
}
```

## Rule 9: Module Organization

**Description:** Organize modules following the feature-based structure with clear separation of concerns.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
// users/
//   ├── dtos/
//   ├── submodule/
//   ├── users.services.ts
//   ├── users.resolvers.ts
//   └── users.module.ts
```

**Bad Example:**
```typescript
// src/
//   ├── dtos/
//   ├── entities/
//   ├── services/
//   └── resolvers/
```

## Rule 10: Test-Driven Development

**Description:** Write tests for all components, following a structured approach with separate unit, integration, and e2e tests.

**Good Example:**

```typescript
// users.service.spec.ts
describe('UsersService', () => {
  let service: UsersService;
  let repository: MockType<UserRepository>;

  beforeEach(async () => {
    const module: TestingModule = await Test.createTestingModule({
      providers: [
        UsersService,
        {
          provide: UserRepository,
          useFactory: createMockRepository,
        },
      ],
    }).compile();

    service = module.get<UsersService>(UsersService);
    repository = module.get(UserRepository);
  });

  describe('findOne', () => {
    it('should return a user', async () => {
      const user = { id: '123', name: 'Test User' };
      repository.findOne.mockReturnValue(user);
      expect(await service.findOne('123')).toEqual(user);
      expect(repository.findOne).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ where: { id: '123' } });
    });
  });
});
```

**Bad Example:**

```typescript
// No tests written, or only testing happy paths
```

