## Rule 16: DTO Organization and Scope

**Description:** When creating a new module, organize DTOs in separate files by operation type (create, update, etc.) and limit their usage to within the module they are defined.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
// membership/dtos/plan.dto.ts
@InputType()
export class CreatePlanDto {
  @Field(() => String)
  @IsNotEmpty()
  @IsString()
  name: string;
}

@ArgsType()
export class CreatePlanArgs {
  @Field(() => CreatePlanDto)
  @Type(() => CreatePlanDto)
  @ValidateNested()
  data: CreatePlanDto;
}

// membership/dtos/update-plan.dto.ts
@InputType()
export class UpdatePlanDto {
  @Field(() => String, { nullable: true })
  @IsOptional()
  @IsString()
  name?: string;
}

@ArgsType()
export class UpdatePlanArgs {
  @Field(() => String)
  @IsNotEmpty()
  @IsString()
  id: string;

  @Field(() => UpdatePlanDto)
  @Type(() => UpdatePlanDto)
  @ValidateNested()
  data: UpdatePlanDto;
}

// These DTOs are only used within the membership module
```

**Bad Example:**
```typescript
// shared/dtos/plan.dto.ts
@InputType()
export class PlanDto {
  // Mixed create and update fields in one DTO
  @Field(() => String, { nullable: true })
  id?: string;
  
  @Field(() => String)
  name: string;
  
  @Field(() => String, { nullable: true })
  description?: string;
}

// Using this DTO across multiple modules
```

**Guidelines for DTO Organization:**
1. Create separate files for each operation type (create, update, get, delete)
2. Keep DTOs scoped to their module and don't use them across different modules
3. Apply proper validation to each field according to the operation type
4. For complex operations, consider creating specialized DTOs
5. Follow the naming convention: `<Operation><Entity>Dto` (e.g., CreateUserDto, UpdateUserDto)

